ter  the last people returned from the moon  in 1972  nobody no human has been  further  from earth than in low earth orbit  mainly in the space station and this is  really because  there's no practical need to send people  especially now that robots are getting  better  well maybe that's something that would  change as it looks like we're set to go  into a cold war with china in the next  few decades i mean china's got its  designs on the moon it's got its changa  missions um and  we already see a tech arms race between  china and the us on things to do with um  technology on earth so i wonder if that  space race do you think it will restart  well i think there may be a space race  um perhaps to uh  put  facilities on the moon but whether they  will involve humans  is not so clear to me and the chinese  might decide for reasons of uh  national pride to send people to the  moon or they might even decide to send  humans to mars because if they want to  assert their parity with the us it  wouldn't be enough to do what the  americans had done 50 years earlier so  they might decide to do these reasons  but um other than that  i don't think there's much case because  uh robots can now do the kind of things  which humans were needed for in the past  and of course if we're thinking about  mars  it's hugely much more expensive to send  humans and preserve them safely on a  200-day voyage than it is to send a  robot that's a hibernates on the way and  you need to bring back so my line in the  article and more generally is that  the case for humans  is just  as an adventure  exploration  rather like the people who  explored the southern hemisphere in the  17th century and the polar explorers etc  and  i think this should be left to private  enterprise  because  public  funding in the u.s and in europe has to  be very risk-averse if you're sending  civilians then you've got to ensure  they're safe the public won't accept a  high failure rate for instance the  shuttle  which send people up to the space  station was launched 135 times  and there were two failures  each of which killed the crew of seven  and those were traumatic events for the  americans  because they've been presented as safe  tourism as it were  but of course a less than two percent  failure rate  which is what that represents is  entirely acceptable to test pilots or um  adventurers and so  i think that we should leave this  venture of human uh space flights um to  uh mrs musk and bezos and other sponsors  um because they can  launch thrill seekers prepared to accept  high risks  and  they will go to mars perhaps some of  them one day tickets in fact  elon musk himself has said he wants to  die on mars but not an impact and he's  51 years old now so 40 years from now  good luck to him  absolutely well do you think that there  is a risk in letting private  entrepreneurs take the lead on this  insofar as  let's let's entertain this sci-fi  scenario where human race has colonized  other planets perhaps the moon um if we  let the first mover be private  individuals who are not elected who  represent the human race who are not  representative in any way themselves  because they're uber rich and have very  uh  idiosyncratic interests you know can  they be trusted to  make that first move there'll be lots of  resources that they could tap into give  them incredible power um isn't it better  in the hands of governments for that to  be happening  well my cynical answer will be can you  trust the americans  the uh i think a more realistic answer  is we're talking about just a few  pioneers and i think it's a dangerous  delusion which is held by elon musk in  particular that  there will be millions of people who  will want to go to mars to escape the  earth's problems that's a dangerous  illusion because although it's hard to  deal with climate change and all that  it's far harder  to terraform mars to make it as  habitable as the top of everest or the  ocean bed and so i don't ever see there  being mass  migration so i don't really uh worry  about  mars being heavily populated and those  sorts of issues arising i think we need  to worry about that for the moment on  the other hand i do say in the article  that i  um cheer these people on  because they will be away from the  regulators  who on earth are going quite rightly to  regulate the use of  cyborg and genetic technology  to modify human beings  uh  we don't want that to happen on the  earth we've got to regulate it but these  guys on mars they'll be away from the  clutches of the regulators moreover they  will have every incentive  to adapt themselves because they're very  poorly adapted to the gravity the  atmosphere and everything else on mars  and so my scenario  is that those crazy guys on mars  will pioneer what i'd call post-human  evolution they'll end up being a new  species because they will evolve um  at a rate what i call secular  intelligent design which is a much much  faster than dominion selection  and they'll be a different species and  they may at some stage become purely  electronic  in which case they might want to uh  leave a planet and go off into the blue  yonder and if they're near immortal then  they won't be deterred by a long  entertainer voyages so the far future  could be dominated by those entities and  one thing which as an astronomer i'm  probably more ever aware of than most  people is that  we are near the beginning than the end  of evolution it's taken about four  billion years to get from the first  embryonic life on earth to the biosphere  which we're apart  but the sun's  less than halfway through its life it's  got six billion more years to go before  it flows up and dies and the wider  cosmos may go on far longer so  in those  thousands of  certainly millions or billions of years  ahead then we can imagine changes which  would be at least as dramatic as the  ones that have happened through  darwinian evolution and so i think we  have no idea what  will happen on those vast time spans  but  i think we can say that this century is  rather special  which is the first century when  one species on one planet namely us here  on earth can actually uh uh trigger  these developments and have to  transition from flesh and blood to  electronic entities  do you think that in those billions of  years  something like climate change wouldn't  end the human race before then  in a sense that that would prompt us to  leave earth on mass  well i mean i hope we can deal with  climate change in the next hundred years  um but i think um even on earth  i don't think humans will survive  looking like humans for um  even one million years wow  and um and so uh life may have a longer  future on earth but the point is that we  are adapted by dominion selection  um to live on this earth and we cherish  it and be admired it's beautiful and we  don't want to change it too fast so i'd  be in favor of regulation  to limit the rate at which  our  earth and our physique and our  mentality can be changed by these new  technologies and hope we can preserve  the earth um in the face of the obvious  challenges which are already concerning  as you know because there are more of us  on the earth all more demanding of  ancient resources and all changing the  climate in a way that hasn't happened  in such a big way over any previous  century  martin i notice you you you say these  entities when you're talking about these  far in the distance um  descendants of us do you think that they  will still be human  um  well no i mean i i i think  well  i i think if we talk about the kind of  evolution it's going to happen away from  the earth  on mars or far beyond um they'll be as  different from us as we are from slime  mold  people  because the time for evolution  between uh  us and them is as long as the time is  taken for us to evolve from slime mold  and moreover duty evolutionary faster  because that will happen on a  technological time scale  which certainly is now when we say what  50 years or so uh whereas darwinian  evolution takes hundreds of thousands of  years  so we've no idea  what these entities would be in the far  future it's very exciting times and very  exciting to think about and martin just  finally before i let you go something  else that we must talk about is if human  if the human race is going to go into  the galaxy even more even beyond  chances of encountering other  intelligent life perhaps i mean in those  pictures from nasa uh this week we saw  that possibly there were some galaxies  that held water or life um  circumstances for life to develop so i  mean is that do you think an  inevitability that we will meet other  alien species well i'd like to say bell  is a harder subject in astronomy and so  we can't make confident predictions  about that but uh it's one of the most  important questions in science of course  um whether life was something which only  originated because of some rare fluke on  the earth or whether it's something was  would have started on many  environments like the earth and we now  know that there are many such  environments like the young earth on  planets orbiting other stars and uh one  reason why i'm excited about the james  webb telescope  is that i think uh it may well provide  the first clues uh to um whether there's  any sort of vegetation or biosphere on  any of those planets and that would at  least tell us that the origin of simple  life was something this wasn't a rare  fluke um as to the likelihood that that  would develop into something  intelligent which we could recognize as  such uh that of course is even more  uncertain but i think it's worth looking  and i think if we look at the  instruments that we'll have in the next  20 or 30 years even more powerful than  james webb  bigger telescopes on the ground which  european scientists are building for  instance  these will be able to clarify  whether there's any life and of course  if there are aliens out there  then  it's worth  a search and there are programs to look  for evidence for anything that's a  manifestly artificial  type of artifacts or to look for any  transmission that doesn't look natural  doesn't come to any kind of star  anything like that  and this is a search which has been  pursued with some private support in  particular um a uh russian-american  investor called yuri milner  is putting 10 million dollars a year for  10 years into a program and i chair his  advisory board and i'm very positive  about this which is simply going to uh  use existing radio telescopes  put some special instruments on them so  that they can be optimized to look for  transmissions that have unusual  properties which might be an indicator  of some sort of  intelligence or be relics of some  intelligence which existed in the past  martin rhys lord ruiz thank you so much  for joining spectator tv this really  puts the tory leadership contest into  context  right  that's it for this week  remember if you enjoy what we do here at  the spectator you can subscribe to the  magazine and to our online content join  today and you'll pay just one pound a  week for unlimited online and app access  in your first year  go to spectator dot co dot uk forward  slash tv offer  and don't forget you can subscribe for  free to the spectator's youtube channel  just click the subscribe button at the  bottom of this video and tap the bell  icon so you never miss an episode  thanks again for watching and do join us  next week  [Music]  you
 

